## Interview-Ready One-Liners

### "What is CAP theorem?"
> "In a distributed system, when a network partition occurs, you must choose between consistency — all nodes see the same data — and availability — every request gets a response. Partition tolerance is non-negotiable since networks fail."

### "Why can't you have all three?"
> "During a partition, a node either serves potentially stale data (availability) or refuses to respond until it can confirm freshness (consistency). You can't do both simultaneously."

### "How do you decide between CP and AP?"
> "Ask: would it be catastrophic if users briefly saw inconsistent data? If yes — like double-booking a seat — choose consistency. If no — like seeing a stale profile picture — choose availability."

### "What about systems that need both?"
> "Real systems use mixed consistency. Ticketmaster needs strong consistency for booking but availability for browsing. You choose per-feature, not globally."

### "What's eventual consistency?"
> "The system will converge to a consistent state if you stop writing, but there's no guarantee about when. It buys you the fastest writes and highest availability at the cost of potentially stale reads."

